WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:02.520
Why do the hot deserts sit in

2
00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:03.440
a belt?

3
00:00:03.440 --> 00:00:04.910
Blame the air.

4
00:00:05.357 --> 00:00:06.897
At the equator the sun is

5
00:00:06.897 --> 00:00:09.477
strong. Air heats and rises by

6
00:00:09.477 --> 00:00:11.337
convection, leaving a low

7
00:00:11.337 --> 00:00:13.217
pressure, the Inter Tropical

8
00:00:13.217 --> 00:00:14.667
Convergence Zone.

9
00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:17.160
The air climbs to the top of

10
00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:19.830
the troposphere, up to fourteen

11
00:00:19.830 --> 00:00:21.920
kilometres, and moves towards

12
00:00:21.920 --> 00:00:23.050
the poles.

13
00:00:23.619 --> 00:00:25.299
It piles up around thirty

14
00:00:25.299 --> 00:00:27.439
degrees north and south.

15
00:00:27.439 --> 00:00:29.479
Part of it sinks, and cooling

16
00:00:29.479 --> 00:00:30.919
air sinks too.

17
00:00:30.919 --> 00:00:32.939
That makes the subtropical

18
00:00:32.939 --> 00:00:33.749
highs.

19
00:00:34.322 --> 00:00:36.642
Sinking air means few clouds.

20
00:00:36.642 --> 00:00:38.802
The most insolation reaches the

21
00:00:38.802 --> 00:00:40.522
ground over the subtropical

22
00:00:40.522 --> 00:00:42.522
deserts, where cloudiness is

23
00:00:42.522 --> 00:00:45.712
least. Clear skies, no shade.

24
00:00:46.164 --> 00:00:48.544
Near the ground, the air flows

25
00:00:48.544 --> 00:00:50.344
back to the equator as the

26
00:00:50.344 --> 00:00:52.984
easterlies. The loop closes.

27
00:00:52.984 --> 00:00:55.524
This tropical cell is called the

28
00:00:55.524 --> 00:00:56.794
Hadley cell.

29
00:00:57.269 --> 00:00:59.749
So the great hot deserts sit

30
00:00:59.749 --> 00:01:02.209
under these dry highs, from the

31
00:01:02.209 --> 00:01:04.859
Sahara to the Arabian desert.

32
00:01:06.128 --> 00:01:06.881
For GS1:

33
00:01:06.881 --> 00:01:09.088
rising air at the ITCZ, sinking

34
00:01:09.088 --> 00:01:13.088
air near thirty degrees, the

35
00:01:13.088 --> 00:01:14.988
subtropical highs, the Hadley

36
00:01:14.988 --> 00:01:16.688
cell, and the hot deserts

37
00:01:16.688 --> 00:01:17.938
beneath them.

38
00:01:18.738 --> 00:01:20.498
Up at the equator.

39
00:01:20.498 --> 00:01:22.448
Down in the desert.
